{
  "case_id": "2016-16-32",
  "document_hash": "bbe37c5c6d03312823972ba214b15589adef02c13609deec19611cfac69bc14f",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2016-16-32",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbe37c5c6d03312823972ba214b15589adef02c13609deec19611cfac69bc14f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbe37c5c6d03-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:22 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2016-16-32",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbe37c5c6d03312823972ba214b15589adef02c13609deec19611cfac69bc14f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbe37c5c6d03-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: Suppose Kentucky had a statute providing that an attorney-in-fact lacks authority to do a -- a -- a number of things: Convey"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2016-16-32",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbe37c5c6d03312823972ba214b15589adef02c13609deec19611cfac69bc14f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbe37c5c6d03-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. PINCUS: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: To determine whether a power of attorney confers authority to enter into an arbitration agreement, Kentucky applies the rule different and more demanding than the rule for determining whether a power of attorney confers authority to enter into contracts generally."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondents",
      "case_id": "2016-16-32",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbe37c5c6d03312823972ba214b15589adef02c13609deec19611cfac69bc14f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbe37c5c6d03-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 18,
        "start": 18
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SALYER: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: Kentucky is a common law State in the development of its power of attorney interpretive law. It is not a uniform power of attorney act State."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2016-16-32",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbe37c5c6d03312823972ba214b15589adef02c13609deec19611cfac69bc14f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbe37c5c6d03-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: So what's the difference between that and the case we have, where we're not going to have a mediator, we're not going to have settlement, we're not going to have a judge trial, we're going to have an arbitrator."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2016-16-32",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbe37c5c6d03312823972ba214b15589adef02c13609deec19611cfac69bc14f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbe37c5c6d03-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: So -- and your reasoning, I take it, that Kentucky could adopt a law that says agents lack authority to enter into arbitration on behalf of the principal."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2016-16-32",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbe37c5c6d03312823972ba214b15589adef02c13609deec19611cfac69bc14f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbe37c5c6d03-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 32,
        "start": 32
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Is there any case that suggests that -- except this case, which happens to deal with arbitration."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2016-16-32",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbe37c5c6d03312823972ba214b15589adef02c13609deec19611cfac69bc14f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbe37c5c6d03-0001-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 35,
        "start": 35
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: But what about the principle that making an arbitration agreement harder to form than other agreements violates the Federal Arbitration Act."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2016-16-32",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbe37c5c6d03312823972ba214b15589adef02c13609deec19611cfac69bc14f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbe37c5c6d03-0002-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "uncertain",
      "text": "So in summation, our -- our general -- our -- our general position is that the Federal Arbitration Act again does not extend this Court's interpretive powers beyond agreements to arbitrate. It does not extend them to powers of attorney. However, this Court need not even reach out to that particular principle because we are talking about interpreting a power of attorney, which, in the ordinary course of a State instrument, is up to the State supreme court to -- to -- to make that interpretation. And this Court will only step in and substitute its interpretation for that of the State supreme court in the exceptional circumstances."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Andrew J. Pincus (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2016-16-32",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbe37c5c6d03312823972ba214b15589adef02c13609deec19611cfac69bc14f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbe37c5c6d03-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "With respect to the -- the question about whether the FAA is limited to arbitration agreements, I think it's important to note that the text of the FAA says that arbitration agreements shall be valid irrevocable, and enforceable, save upon such grounds as exist at law or in equity for the revocation of any contract."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2016-16-32",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbe37c5c6d03312823972ba214b15589adef02c13609deec19611cfac69bc14f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbe37c5c6d03-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The State could also enact a statute that says powers of attorney can't waive -- can't waive fundamental rights. Would -- would that violate the FAA, in your view?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Andrew J. Pincus (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2016-16-32",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbe37c5c6d03312823972ba214b15589adef02c13609deec19611cfac69bc14f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbe37c5c6d03-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "that they have to prove it. I think this Court has to look at the objective indicia, as it did in Imburgia, as it's done in other cases, to see what -- what is really going on in a case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2016-16-32",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbe37c5c6d03312823972ba214b15589adef02c13609deec19611cfac69bc14f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbe37c5c6d03-0002-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 50,
        "start": 50
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    },
